{
  "name": "certracept",

  /* THE ENTRY POINT MUST BE THE FILE, NOT THE `_worker.js` DIRECTORY, AND THE
     MODULES BESIDE IT MUST BE UPLOADED WITH IT.

     next-on-pages emits `_worker.js/index.js` plus a tree of route functions
     under `_worker.js/__next-on-pages-dist__/`, which index.js reaches by
     dynamic import at request time. Cloudflare PAGES understands that layout
     natively; a Worker deploy does not — it uploads exactly the modules the
     config tells it to. Pointing `main` at the directory uploaded index.js
     alone (108 KiB, zero additional modules), so the moment a request needed a
     route function the Worker threw

         No such module "__next-on-pages-dist__/functions/middleware.func.js"

     and answered 500. That is every dynamic route — /declarations/[id],
     /w/[token] and the other Smart Link entries, /workspaces/[id]/*,
     /suppliers/[id] — plus every 404, which surfaced as a 500 too. It also
     took middleware.ts down with it, so the nonce-based CSP that is supposed
     to protect Worker-rendered pages never ran.

     `base_dir` + `no_bundle` + an ESModule rule uploads the whole tree
     (78 modules) and hands the runtime the graph index.js actually imports.
     `no_bundle` is right here because next-on-pages has already bundled this
     output; re-bundling it would only break the dynamic import paths again.

     Prerendered pages never touch any of this: Cloudflare serves them from
     the asset layer below, with the headers in public/_headers. */
  "main": ".vercel/output/static/_worker.js/index.js",
  "base_dir": ".vercel/output/static/_worker.js",
  "no_bundle": true,
  "rules": [
    { "type": "ESModule", "globs": ["**/*.js"] }
  ],

  "compatibility_date": "2024-09-23",
  "compatibility_flags": [
    "nodejs_compat"
  ],
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".vercel/output/static",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  }

  /* No `vars` block on purpose. Everything this app reads is NEXT_PUBLIC_*,
     which Next inlines into the bundle during the build — a Worker var arrives
     at request time, long after that bundle was frozen, so it can never change
     the value the code sees. NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_MODE sat here reading as the
     demo switch while having no effect at all. The switch is the default in
     next.config.ts; the build env is in .github/workflows/deploy.yml. */
}
